In this lesson we'll look at how counting techniques plug straight into probability. Whenever every outcome of an experiment is equally likely, probability is just one simple idea — favourable over total. The trick is that listing every outcome gets messy fast, so we use combinations to count for us. Think of combinations as the number of ways to pick a group where order doesn't matter. For "selecting without regard to order", we put combinations on the top to count the favourable groups, and combinations on the bottom to count every possible group of that size. So the probability of an event becomes combinations of the favourable choices, divided by combinations of all possible choices of that same size. Picture a bag with red and blue counters — the chance of drawing two reds is combinations of reds taken two at a time, sitting over combinations of the whole bag taken two at a time. Same structure every time — pick the size, count the good groups, count all the groups, divide. Now let's see it in action.